Implement `GET /api/reports/export.csv?from=YYYY-MM-DD&to=YYYY-MM-DD` endpoint in Rust using axum. Query SQLite database via sqlx to fetch time entries filtered by date range and current local user scope. Include columns: entry_id, date, start_time, end_time, duration_seconds, duration_formatted, description, project_name, client_name, tags (comma-separated), billable, hourly_rate, currency, amount. Use the `csv` crate (e.g., `csv = "1.3"`) to serialize rows. Return CSV with proper Content-Type and Content-Disposition headers. Ensure column names are stable and match import expectations. Handle timezone conversions appropriately for local user context.

Pseudo-code:
```rust
#[get("/api/reports/export.csv")]
async fn export_csv(query: Query<ExportParams>, db: Extension<SqlitePool>) -> Result<Response> {
  let entries = sqlx::query_as::<_, TimeEntryRow>(
    "SELECT e.id, e.date, e.start_time, e.end_time, e.duration_seconds, e.description, 
     p.name as project_name, c.name as client_name, e.billable, p.hourly_rate, p.currency 
     FROM time_entries e LEFT JOIN projects p ON e.project_id = p.id 
     LEFT JOIN clients c ON p.client_id = c.id 
     WHERE e.user_id = ? AND e.date >= ? AND e.date <= ?"
  ).fetch_all(&db).await?;

  let mut wtr = csv::Writer::from_writer(vec![]);
  for entry in entries {
    wtr.serialize(entry)?;
  }
  let csv_data = wtr.into_inner()?;

  Ok(Response::builder()
    .header("Content-Type", "text/csv")
    .header("Content-Disposition", "attachment; filename=\"animo_export.csv\"")
    .body(csv_data)?)
}
```

